# Code ownership, expressed against the directory layout.
#
# This file is the reason `modules/<name>/` exists. Under the old layout a
# team's work was spread across `packages/features/auth`,
# `packages/domain/auth` and `packages/data/auth`, and CODEOWNERS cannot say
# "the auth rows of three sibling directories" — it matches paths. One
# directory per bounded context makes ownership one line per team.
#
# Replace the placeholder handles below with real GitHub teams. A team that
# does not exist is silently ignored by GitHub, which means the rule looks
# enforced and is not — so do this before relying on it.
#
# Later rules win, so the order here is deliberate: broad defaults first,
# specific ownership after.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default — anything not claimed below
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                               @your-org/maintainers

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform — the infra team's ground.
#
# Every module depends on this, so a breaking change here breaks everyone at
# once. That asymmetry, not seniority, is why it has a dedicated owner.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/platform/                      @your-org/infra

# `platform/di` holds every cross-module contract. Changing one is a
# negotiation between modules, not a unilateral edit, so it gets its own rule.
/platform/di/                   @your-org/infra @your-org/architects

# The shared app shell. Infra maintains it, but every app boots through it, so
# the people who own the apps review changes to it too.
/platform/app_shell/            @your-org/infra @your-org/tech-leads

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Modules — one vertical slice, one owning team.
#
# A module owns all three of its layers. The team that changes the UI is the
# team that changes the use case behind it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/modules/auth/                  @your-org/team-auth
/modules/home/                  @your-org/team-home
/modules/settings/              @your-org/team-home
/modules/dashboard/             @your-org/team-shell
/modules/onboarding/            @your-org/team-shell
/modules/splash/                @your-org/team-shell

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apps — the composition layer, owned by tech leads.
#
# An app decides which modules ship together and in what order they
# initialise. That is a release decision, not a feature decision.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/apps/                          @your-org/tech-leads

# The manifest IS the composition. Adding a module here changes what the
# product is, so it is reviewed separately from the rest of the app shell.
/apps/*/app_manifest.yaml       @your-org/tech-leads @your-org/architects

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build, CI and the rules themselves
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/tools/                         @your-org/infra
/.github/                       @your-org/infra
/pubspec.yaml                   @your-org/infra
/pubspec_dependencies.yaml      @your-org/infra
/analysis_options.yaml          @your-org/infra
/fastlane/                      @your-org/release
/apps/*/fastlane/               @your-org/release

# The architecture rules and the plan that produced them.
/.agents/                       @your-org/architects
/CLAUDE.md                      @your-org/architects
/docs/                          @your-org/architects
